WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.240
Network and remote access artifacts help analysts understand how a Linux system communicated and how users reached it.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.820
SSH is often the most important remote access service.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:19.040
Authentication logs may show accepted and failed logins, source addresses, users, session starts, and sudo activity after login.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:26.540
Authorized_keys files can show which public keys were trusted for an account, while known_hosts may show systems the account connected to.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.200
Listening services and network configuration provide context.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:40.820
Analysts may review configured services, active listening ports, firewall rules, routing information, DNS settings, network manager records, and service-specific configuration.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.720
The goal is to understand what exposure was expected and whether observed activity fits the system's purpose.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:55.260
Web access logs, reverse proxy logs, load balancer records, and application logs can help connect Linux host evidence to user activity.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:06.040
In cloud environments, host evidence should often be correlated with cloud audit logs, flow logs, security group changes, load balancer logs, and instance metadata considerations.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.980
A cloud instance may not contain every network artifact needed to explain an event.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.480
Remote access analysis should be careful about attribution.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.820
A source IP address, SSH key, or username may support a timeline, but it rarely proves a person's intent by itself.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:29.240
Shared accounts, jump hosts, VPNs, automation, and cloud management tools can all complicate interpretation.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:36.900
Strong analysis correlates remote access records with account artifacts, process activity, file changes, and business context.
